    <!-- SUBSECTION 2.4: ARCHETYPE 4 DEEP-DIVE -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <div id="archetype-4-deepdive" class="concept-stream" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <div class="concept-step-card archetype-yellow-border">
        <div class="step-card-header">
          <span class="step-tag badge-yellow">2.4 ARCHITECTURAL DEEP-DIVE</span>
          <h3 class="step-card-title">Archetype 4: High-Performance Out-of-Order Superscalar Engine (BOOM / SonicBOOM)</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="step-card-lead">
          Developed at UC Berkeley, <strong>BOOM (Berkeley Out-of-Order Machine / SonicBOOM)</strong> is the pinnacle of open-source RISC-V performance. Inspired by modern commercial server microarchitectures (Cortex-A76, Apple Firestorm, Intel Golden Cove), BOOM shatters the single-issue in-order performance ceiling by executing up to 3 to 4 instructions per cycle out-of-order, renaming registers onto a massive physical register file, and aggressively overlapping DRAM misses with an 8-entry non-blocking MSHR queue.
        </p>

        <!-- 2.4.1 BOOM Technical Breakdown -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-yellow">CORE 4</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">SonicBOOM &bull; 3-Wide Out-of-Order Superscalar Engine</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">~420 kGE &bull; Chisel HDL</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            BOOM deploys the full arsenal of modern superscalar microarchitectural techniques to maximize single-thread IPC:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Multi-Wide Superscalar Execution:</strong> Fetches 4 instructions per clock cycle and decodes/dispatches 3 micro-ops (uOPs) simultaneously into parallel execution pipelines (Dual Integer ALUs, Pipelined Multiplier/Divider, IEEE-754 FPU, and Load/Store AGUs).</li>
            <li><strong>Register Renaming onto Explicit PRF:</strong> Employs a Register Alias Table (RAT) that maps the 32 architectural RISC-V registers onto a 96 to 128-entry unified Physical Register File (PRF). This completely eliminates false Write-After-Read (WAR) and Write-After-Write (WAW) hazards, freeing instructions to execute as soon as true RAW data is produced.</li>
            <li><strong>Unified &amp; Distributed Issue Windows:</strong> Features reservation stations where waiting uOPs continuously snoop execution result broadcast buses. When source operands become valid, the issue logic dispatches ready uOPs speculatively into available execution units with zero wait states.</li>
            <li><strong>Non-Blocking L1-D Cache with 8-Entry MSHR Queue:</strong> Solves the Memory Wall by allowing up to 8 independent DRAM cache line misses to be in-flight concurrently ("misses-under-miss" and "hits-under-miss"). The core continues processing independent compute threads even during prolonged DRAM delays.</li>
            <li><strong>Circular Reorder Buffer (RoB) &amp; Speculative Recovery:</strong> A 64 to 128-entry circular Reorder Buffer holds speculative instruction outcomes. It commits up to 4 instructions per cycle strictly in program order. In the event of a branch misprediction or page fault, the RoB recovers the precise architectural state within a single clock cycle.</li>
          </ul>

          <!-- Figure 2.4: BOOM Datapath SVG Schematic -->
          <div class="schematic-container">
            <div class="schematic-header">
              <span class="schematic-tag">FIGURE 2.4 ARCHITECTURAL SCHEMATIC</span>
              <h4 class="schematic-title">SonicBOOM: 3-Wide Out-of-Order Superscalar Datapath, PRF &amp; MSHR Architecture</h4>
